# MOB RULING — clojure condition retention after the Round-3 identity finding

Read-only: evidence reviewed in full — the astra r3-identity-confirm log (final report + probes), the argon r3-fix-confirm log, and the code at `c9c5533c` (worktree `datomworld-r3-orch`), `master 887cc273`, `yin/vm/engine.cljc`, `values.cljc`, `data.cljc`, ruling v2, the R4 brief, §8.10.7/§8.10.9/§8.10.12.

## Root diagnosis

The wrapper was **over-applied**. Pre-R3 master (`887cc273`, `throw` lowering at `clojure.cljc:1125`) already delivered the ordinary thrown payload **raw** into the error slot, and the catch binder bound the slot — so the caught value simply *was* the payload, and identity held under *every* guest observation, which is exactly why Round 0's `[:clojure :python :round-trip]` was green on all three lanes. R3 introduced the wrapper cell for a real need — protocol-condition retention — but `condition->outcome` (`clojure.cljc:1654-1682` at `c9c5533c`) wrapped **ordinary `:yang.call/thrown` payloads too**, and then tried to reconstruct identity by per-call-site unwinding (`dsg__visible_of` on `=`/`get`/keyword/predicate heads, `:1281-1286`, `:1815-1832`). A syntactic, shallow rewrite can never be total: nested positions, map keys, `count`/`assoc`/truthiness, and any higher-order crossing (`((fn [p] (p e)) vector?)`) see the wrapper. Astra's finding (b) is structural, as the prompt concedes. The answer is not more routing; it is that **the wrapper must never occupy the caught-value position for ordinary payloads** — and, symmetrically, that **contents authentication alone can never own retention** (finding (a)): the witness proves the triple `[visible condition]` is genuine, but not that *this cell* is the retained one, so `cell/new`-replay and `cell/set!`-swap both forward what they should not.

## The design — (A), three commitments

**C1 — Ordinary thrown payloads are delivered raw; the catch binds the slot.** In `condition->outcome`, the `:yang.call/thrown` arm (`:1660-1664`) and the no-qualified-reason arm (`:1679-1682`) stop minting `dsg__condition_wrapper` and put `:yang.call/value` (resp. the payload) directly in the error outcome. The try binder (`:1246-1250`) stays as master had it. Every (b) leak closes **by construction**: `e` *is* the payload, so `(= [e] [orig])`, `(get {orig :found} e :missing)`, `(count e)`, `(assoc e :a 2)`, caught-`nil` falsity, aliased predicates, and cross-language passage all answer as the payload with zero routing — and the error path mints **no cell and no witness at all**, which is a net reduction toward the slice-5 purity bar, not growth. The existing routing sets stay but become belt-and-suspenders that now only ever fire on protocol forms.

**C2 — The wrapper survives only as the documented local form for qualified protocol conditions.** `:yang.call/not-callable`, `:invalid-invocation`, `:unsupported-binding` (the arms at `:1674-1676`, `:1699-1702`, `:1719-1722`, `:2806`) keep the wrapper, because the `saved-protocol-*` rows require provenance to travel *with a saved value across activations* — no lexical catch-site state can do that, and R5 forbids recognizing the condition map by shape on rethrow. There the form is opaque-with-accessors (`ex-message`/`ex-data`/`(:yang.call/reason e)` via the existing routing), exactly the stance python/php take with `ForeignException`. §R2(1) has no antecedent here: the guest never held the condition map as a value to be restored — the form is *generated*, and "ordinary catching and identity behavior" for it is what the local form documents. Payload identity (the round-trip bar) is a C1 matter and is total.

**C3 — Identity-bound retention closes (a): the issuer marks the wrapper; contents freeze.** The witness authenticates contents but not the cell's identity as the retained one — and per §8.10.12's own logic, the distinguishing fact ("did the issuer mint *this* cell as a retained-condition carrier?") "is not in the value. It is in the heap. Only the issuer can answer." §8.10.7 already names the home: "an equivalent task-owned identity structure with the same lifetime," no ambient registry. Concretely: a compiler-internal mint (unpublished, like the `dsg__call_token` machinery) writes a `:retained` mark on the heap entry at `:cell/new`-time (`engine.cljc:2773-2778`); `cell/set!` (`:2785-2790`) refuses marked entries, freezing the triple for the wrapper's lifetime (the refusal rides the module's existing strict-validation convention, as `check-ref!` already does for non-cells); and a pure `cell/retained?`-style query — same family as `cell/authentic?` (`:2796-2799`) — is added so the lowered decoder `dsg__condition_retained` can require it. Replay `(throw (cell/new (cell/get e)))` then lands in an *unmarked* guest cell: decode fails, and it is delivered as the payload of a **fresh** `:yang.call/thrown` — precisely the R5 `forged-retention` semantics. Mutation `(cell/set! e (cell/get other))` refuses at the op. This also answers the prompt's side-table question: the table is keyed by **the cell id** — and it is not a table at all but a bit on the heap entry that id already names, so its lifetime is the wrapper's, exactly as §8.10.7 demands. With mark + freeze the witness closure becomes redundant and can be retired — one fewer mint on the error path.

**How `throw e` still forwards the condition.** Ordinary: `(throw e)` is a fresh `:yang.call/thrown` with `e` = the payload — byte-identical to the original mint for source-thrown values, and payload-identical (what every ordinary row asserts: `:reason :yang.call/thrown`, `:payload-identical true`) for foreign-arrived ones; evidence-completeness obligations attach to *protocol* conditions, which C2 retains byte-for-byte through `call-entry`/`strip-top-outcome` (`:1428-1438`, `:2599-2625`) as the P1-3 fix already established. Protocol: `e` *is* the marked wrapper; the boundary decodes (mark + frozen contents) and forwards the stored condition unchanged, including from another activation via `later`.

## Why this is not the R4 failure (B rejected)

The astra finding is a **uniform defect in what the catch binds**, not a divergence among the three lanes: `condition->outcome` and the binder are shared by the intra-unit packet lane, the outcome-token tail lane, and the public bridge, so C1+C2 land on all three at once and the §8.10.9 equivalence proof (the staged R4 brief) becomes *tractable* — after the patch, the guest-visible meaning of the error slot no longer depends on the channel. The continuation lane would deliver **the same caught value** (the payload) with the same fresh-throw rethrow semantics, and it would **still need C3** — `saved-protocol-*` and retained-lifetime obligations are lane-independent, and clojure-in-yin has no host exception objects to ride identity on; the marked cell is the only identity-bearing carrier on either lane. So the port buys no contract compliance the patch lacks, costs a full re-lowering of the call machinery across three lanes plus cross-unit admission, and forfeits the owner-preferred optimization. The outcome channel *can* deliver payload identity — master proved it before the wrapper regressed it.

## Why the contract is not narrowed (C rejected)

In clojure's runtime **every value is a valid thrown payload**, so §8.10.7 reception rule 1 ("a payload valid as an exception in its own runtime") already covers all ordinary payloads without remainder; the only values outside it are the qualified protocol conditions, which C2 handles as the generated local form the ruling itself prescribes. (C) would redefine `[:clojure :python :round-trip]` to assert accessor-only semantics — blessing a regression of a row Round 0 proved green on master — which is tailoring the ruling to the implementation. (C) is the fallback of last resort for a design vacuum; here a compliant, small design exists.

## What survives of R3; what the patch round must do

Survives: P1-1 (strengthened into C3), P1-3 byte-for-byte condition storage, P2-4 assertions, `ex-message`/`ex-data` accessors and the routing (now protocol-form-only), the refusal arms. Changes: the two ordinary arms of `condition->outcome` (C1), the engine mark + `set!` refusal + retained query (C3 — a bounded `yin/vm` extension beyond the R4 brief's clojure-only scope; it needs its own mini-round, Node/Dart parity, and the four-seat vocabulary note per the §8.10.12 process — note it is *not* a cost of choosing (A): any compliant design, (B) included, requires it). Tests: astra's exact probe table as regressions — nested `(= [e] [orig])`, map-key `get`, `count`, `assoc`-update, caught-`nil` truthiness, aliased predicate, `cell/new` replay, `cell/set!` swap — on both walker and semantic lanes; then R4 proceeds exactly as staged.

**(A)** — the caught value must simply *be* the payload (as pre-R3 master already proved green), with the wrapper demoted to the issuer-marked, frozen, protocol-only local form; the finding indicts the wrapper's placement, not the outcome channel, so the R4 equivalence proof remains winnable and the (B) fallback is not authorized.
